Sure, here is the equivalent JavaScript implementation:

```javascript
function sortMatrix(matrix) {
    let evenRowSum = 0;
    let oddRowSum = 0;

    // Rearrange even rows
    for (let i = 0; i < matrix.length; i++) {
        if (i % 2 != 0) {
            matrix[i] = matrix[i].sort((a, b) => a - b);
            evenRowSum += matrix[i].reduce((a, b) => a + b, 0);
        } else {
            oddRowSum += matrix[i].reduce((a, b) => a + b, 0);
        }
    }

    // If sum of even rows > sum of odd rows, sort all rows
    if (evenRowSum > oddRowSum) {
        for (let i = 0; i < matrix.length; i++) {
            matrix[i] = matrix[i].sort((a, b) => a - b);
        }
    }

    return matrix;
}
```

This script uses JavaScript's array sort() method, which you'll notice compares `a` and `b` values, unlike in Python's sorted() function. The reason for this is that JavaScript's sort() method by default sorts arrays lexicographically. The comparison function `(a, b) => a - b` is needed to sort numbers in numerical order.

Keep in mind that in JavaScript, array indices start with `0`, so the check `i % 2 != 0` sorts the even-indexed rows in the original Python function, but in JavaScript, this sorts the odd-indexed rows instead. However, the results will still be the same because we're considering the parity of the row index, not the row index itself.

